  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nAccording to the specification, when a document initially loads, if multiple elements have the `autofocus` attribute and each has a different nearest ancestor `autofocus` scoping root element, the document remains compliant with the standards. However, which element should actually receive focus in this scenario?\n\nCode example:\n```\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<title>multiple displayed autofocus elements with different nearest ancestor autofocus scoping root element</title>\n<input id=\"input1\" autofocus>\n<dialog open>\n    <input id=\"input2\" autofocus>\n</dialog>\n<dialog open>\n    <input id=\"input3\" autofocus>\n</dialog>\n</html>\n```\n\nI couldn't find any documentation clarifying which element should get focus in this case - I might have missed the relevant section. If this is indeed covered somewhere, please feel free to close this thread.\n\nBTW, this issue can also extend to `<dialog>` elements without `autofocus`, as according to the spec, `<dialog>` has an implicit auto focus feature.",
